  "question": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\qt0069_49ca36f8d478.png\" style=\"width: 500px; height: 149px;\" /></p><p>Compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> is neutral, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math> gives effervescence with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NaHCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> reacts with Hinsberg&rsquo;s reagent to give solid soluble in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi><mo>.</mo></math> Compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> is</p>"
